// Lesson 9.10 — Impact, Ethics & Licenses (Your Fork!)
// expand-app: open source, fork ethics, Apache 2.0 (NOT MIT — the starter
// ships Apache License 2.0), digital divide, attribution.
//
// SHAPE: this is the tenth lesson of Unit 9 and the only one that arrived
// without the recurring three-question block, which made it the one hole in an
// otherwise uniform unit. It is a concept lesson — nothing is built and there is
// no script to hand over — so it uses the `concept` frame (src/lib/walkthrough.ts)
// on both the banner and the block, and sits at level 3, which is the only rung
// reachable for a lesson that renders no walkthrough (check 7). It renders none.
//
// The block is ADDED, not substituted: it opens a new answer-id namespace
// (9.10:dc1:qN) and leaves every protected block in exactly the count and
// document position it already had — socratic 1, bug-hunt 4, tinker 3. (The
// bug-hunt and the tinker were later removed from every lesson, so the socratic
// is the one protected block this page still renders.) Nothing in those blocks
// was reordered, renumbered, added or removed, and nothing in them was reworded
// either: unlike 9.8, every claim in this lesson already held when it was
// checked, so the text is untouched. `checklist` is not harvested by
// formify.ts and is likewise unchanged.
//
// GROUND TRUTH — checked, not assumed:
//   * The Apache claim is TRUE. `AP_CS_P_Starter_Project/LICENSE` exists, is
//     201 lines, and its first line is "Apache License" / "Version 2.0, January
//     2004". The lesson's "this is the Apache License 2.0, not MIT" is correct.
//   * The `LICENSE` is genuinely only in the starter project. The Unit 10 card
//     jam (`AP_CS_Principles/Card-Game-Template-main/`) ships NO license file at
//     all, which is why this lesson's file-reading steps say "your fork's repo"
//     and never point at a path in this repo.
//   * Section numbers cited here (2 for the grant, 4 for the obligations) match
//     that file: section 2 is the copyright grant, section 4 the conditions.
//   * Every reference in `connect` and `turns` points BACKWARD (8.6, 9.5, 9.6,
//     9.7) and none is a forward lesson — the verifier greps this beat and fails
//     on anything the student has not reached.
import Lesson from '../../layouts/LessonLayout.astro';
import { renderAskBanner } from '../../lib/walkthrough';
import { renderDecompose } from '../../lib/decompose';
import type { DecomposeSpec } from '../../lib/decompose';

// The recurring two-beat block: split the problem, look each part up, connect
// it to something already known. Level 3, so the renderer withholds what each
// part DOES and asks the student to say it — the parts and their doc pointers
// are the level-3 bargain. The problem being split is "ship this legally and
// honestly", which is deliberately NOT read as one question: that is the whole
// lesson. Every reference points backwards.
const DECOMPOSE: DecomposeSpec = {
  big: 'You forked somebody else\'s project, built your own game on top of it, and now you want to put it where people can play it. That is not one decision. It is five, and four of them are already settled by a file that arrived with the repo, that you have opened exactly once, and that most people never open again.',
  frame: 'concept',
  parts: [
    {
      name: 'Read what the license actually granted you',
      does: 'The license is the permission slip, and it was handed over the moment you cloned. It says what you may do — run it, change it, publish it, sell it — and it says nobody can revoke that later. You never had to ask anyone.',
      doc: 'section 2 of the `LICENSE` file in your own fork, and the license table in this lesson\'s Concept Lab',
    },
    {
      name: 'Keep the notices',
      does: 'Every copy of the work has to carry the copyright and license notices it came with. It is the cheapest obligation and the one most often skipped, because it feels like packaging rather than law — but a published copy without them is published without permission.',
      doc: 'the "You must" column of this lesson\'s license table',
    },
    {
      name: 'Say out loud which files you changed',
      does: 'This is the clause that makes you announce your edits rather than merely leave them visible in the diff. It is the obligation that surprises people, and it is what the exam\'s attribution questions are really asking about.',
      doc: 'section 4 of your fork\'s `LICENSE`, and the Apache 2.0 row of this lesson\'s license table — "Keep the notice, state your changes, and pass on the patent grant"',
    },
    {
      name: 'Credit every asset you did not make',
      does: 'Your code and your art almost certainly do not carry the same license. A sprite pack, a font or a music loop travels under its own terms, and "free sprites" with no license file is not permission — it is the absence of it.',
      doc: 'the CC BY and CC BY-SA rows of the license table, the CREDITS-file paragraph in this lesson\'s Concept Lab, and the fairness thread from 9.6',
      href: '9.6.html',
    },
    {
      name: 'Decide what the people who play it are owed',
      does: 'Licensing settles what you may do with someone else\'s work. It says nothing about what you do to your own players: what you measure, what you keep, and who your interface silently assumes can see it and can move.',
      doc: 'the digital-divide and data sections of Concept Lab II, and the dataset questions you answered honestly and locally in 9.5',
      href: '9.5.html',
    },
  ],
  why:
    'The split is the whole lesson, because "is my game legal?" has no single answer — it is five questions with five answers, and four of them are yes if you do them and no if you skip them. They are not equally obvious either: the notices one is forgettable, the changes one is genuinely surprising, and the last one is not a license question at all. A student who can tell those five apart will not be caught out by a question asking which concern is most significant, because they will know there was more than one concern on the table.',
  sizeTest:
    'The test for a finished split: can you settle each part by opening one file or naming one sentence? The first three are all settled by the `LICENSE` already sitting in your own fork — section 2 for what you may do and section 4 for what you owe. The fourth is settled by wherever the sprite came from. The fifth cannot be settled by a file at all, which is exactly what makes it the one you decide rather than look up.',
  turns: [
    'People routinely license their code one way and their art another: permissive for the parts they built on, all rights reserved for the parts they drew. Why split it like that, and what does the split let you do that one license covering everything would not?',
    'A permissive license is irreversible. Once you publish under it you cannot take the permission back, and anyone who already took it keeps it forever. What does that make the moment you choose one — and why do people still release under permissive licenses on purpose?',
  ],
};

const body = `<h1>9.10 — Impact, Ethics &amp; Licenses (Your Fork!)</h1>

<p class="lesson-lead">
    Your whole semester is a FOR: you copied someone else's project,
    legally, and made it yours. Today: why that was legal, what you
    owe for it, and the bigger questions — who gets to build games,
    whose data gets taken, and what happens when "the internet" is
    where 8 billion people's lives actually live.
</p>

${renderAskBanner('what do I owe the project I forked?', 3, 'concept')}

${renderDecompose('9.10', DECOMPOSE, 3)}

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity expand-app">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Concept Lab</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~15 min</span>
    </div>

    <p><strong>Open source and your fork.</strong> The starter repo
    ships a LICENSE file — open yours now (your fork's repo, the file
    called <code>LICENSE</code>). Read the first line, because it is
    probably not what you would guess: <strong>this project is under the
    Apache License 2.0</strong>, not MIT.</p>
    <p>Both are <em>permissive</em> licenses — they let you do almost
    anything — but Apache 2.0 is the more formal of the two. Here is the
    phrase from it that does the real work, quoted from section 2 of the
    file you just opened:</p>
    <pre><code>...a perpetual, worldwide, non-exclusive, no-charge,
royalty-free, irrevocable copyright license to reproduce,
prepare Derivative Works of, publicly display, publicly
perform, sublicense, and distribute the Work...</code></pre>
    <p>That one sentence is why your fork was legal, why you may sell
    <a href="8.6.html">8.6</a>'s build on itch.io, and why nobody can
    revoke that permission later. In exchange you owe two things: keep the
    notices, and <em>state that you changed the files</em>. Apache also
    carries an explicit patent grant that MIT has no clause for at all — a
    difference that matters enormously to companies. Not all licenses are
    this permissive:</p>
    <table>
        <tr><th>License</th><th>You may</th><th>You must</th></tr>
        <tr><td>MIT</td><td>Anything, even commercial</td><td>Keep the notice</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Apache 2.0 <em>(your starter)</em></td><td>Anything, even commercial</td><td>Keep the notice, state your changes, and pass on the patent grant</td></tr>
        <tr><td>GPL (copyleft)</td><td>Use and modify freely</td><td>Share YOUR version under GPL too</td></tr>
        <tr><td>CC BY / CC BY-SA (assets)</td><td>Remix art/music</td><td>Credit the author; SA = share alike</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Proprietary / "all rights reserved"</td><td>Nothing without permission</td><td>Ask first</td></tr>
    </table>

    <p><strong>Attribution is the social contract.</strong> Your game
    uses the starter's code and possibly others' sprites. A CREDITS
    file is how the chain of gratitude works — and the exam's
    "impact" questions are exactly this: software is built in
    communities, licenses are their rules of the road.</p>

    <div class="socratic">
        <h3>Socratic</h3>
        <p>
            GPL's copyleft says: fork freely, but your fork must
            stay open. Apache 2.0 — the license you just read —
            says: fork freely, close it if you want. Argue ONE
            side: which produces more good in the world, and why
            would a company pick Apache while Linux picked GPL?
            (There is no right answer; there is an argued answer.)
        </p>
    </div>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity expand-app">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Concept Lab II</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~15 min</span>
    </div>

    <p><strong>Digital divide — who is missing from this room.</strong>
    You build games on school machines with fast wifi. The exam's
    IOC-1 questions ask who is NOT at the table: students without
    home internet (your <a href="9.7.html">9.7</a> minimum spec
    decision is about access, not just fps), people whose language
    the tutorials do not speak, the elderly on interfaces designed
    by 24-year-olds, whole countries whose news feeds run on
    algorithms they cannot audit. Software scales the designer's
    assumptions to millions.</p>

    <p><strong>Your data and the games you play.</strong> Free
    games monetize attention: they measure session time, churn,
    and what makes players return (your own
    <a href="9.5.html">9.5</a> dataset is that measurement, done
    honestly and locally — imagine it sent nightly to a server,
    aggregated across 40 million players, and used to schedule
    "limited-time" events at YOUR 11pm). The exam calls this
    targeted and sometimes predatory design. Knowing the pattern is
    the defense.</p>

    <p><strong>Bias with consequences.</strong> Connect to
    <a href="9.6.html">9.6</a>: a hiring algorithm trained on past
    hires inherits past exclusion; a game tutorial with only one
    skin tone teaches who it expects. When your jam games reach the
    showcase (<a href="10.1f.html">10.1f</a>), your classmates are
    the users — what assumptions did YOU bake in without
    noticing? Name one out loud.</p>

</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->

<div class="checklist">
    <h3>Checkpoint — before moving on, you must be able to:</h3>
    <ul>
        <li>Explain permissive vs copyleft vs proprietary, using your fork's own Apache 2.0 license as the evidence</li>
        <li>Write a correct CREDITS file with sources and licenses</li>
        <li>Describe the digital divide with two concrete examples</li>
        <li>Identify one assumption your own design baked in, and one fix</li>
    </ul>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="resources">
    <h3>Reference Docs</h3>
    <p>Need a deeper explanation? These pages cover the same concepts:</p>
    <ul>
        <li><a href="../docs/unit-09/index.html">Unit 9: Data &amp; Systems</a> — impact in the CED</li>
        <li><a href="../projects.html">Projects</a> — your semester work, the thing you now license</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Next unit: <a href="10.1a.html">10.1a — 2-Button Jam: Kickoff &amp; Pitch</a> — jam season begins.</p>
</div>`;
